What is a Thread?

A thread is the smallest unit of execution inside a process.

A process can contain one or more threads.

All threads within the same process share:

Global variables.
Heap memory.
Open files.
Socket connections.

Each thread has its own:

Stack.
Program Counter (instruction pointer).
CPU registers.

This allows multiple threads to execute different parts of the same program independently.

Why did we need Threads?

Initially, our HTTP server handled one client at a time.

